/*
RMSTEST_22a - elevation seed south bias, WITHOUT enable_balanced_elevation
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 1, Sec.6.2's seed weights

QUESTION
The guide says elevation seed selection is biased toward the south. Sec.6.2
turns that into an integer weight of 100 + 100*y/(dim-1), meaning a tile at the
far south is exactly twice as likely to seed a clump as one at the far north.
The 2x is a [tune] guess. Nothing has ever measured the strength of the bias,
only its direction.

The reason it must be integer weights rather than a float curve is goal 5,
determinism. That makes the constant cheap to change and worth getting right
once, because every future seeded run inherits it.

METHOD
Five hundred one-tile-tall clumps with a 2000 tile budget, so each clump is
about four tiles and the tile distribution is close to the seed distribution
with only a little smearing. Read the mean y as a fraction of the map.

The predictions are far enough apart to separate in a single run.
  uniform seed pick        mean y = 0.500
  Sec.6.2's south bias     mean y = 0.556   (this map)
  enable_balanced_elevation mean y = 0.512  (RMSTEST_22b)

Those come from integrating the weight over the map. Under weight 1 + u with u
uniform on 0 to 1, the expected u is (1/2 + 1/3)/(1 + 1/2), which is 5/9.

NO PLAYER LANDS ON PURPOSE. Sec.6.2 keeps seeds at least 9 tiles from every
player land origin, and those origins sit on a circle, so including them would
carve a ring-shaped hole out of the very distribution being measured. If the
editor refuses to generate a map with no player lands, that refusal is itself
worth recording, and the fallback is to add create_player_lands and treat the
result as contaminated rather than as an answer.

GENERATE AT TINY (120). 2000 tiles of 14400 is 14 percent coverage. Keep it
low deliberately. At high coverage the southern rows saturate, later seeds land
on already-elevated tiles, and the measured mean is dragged back toward 0.500,
which would read as a weaker bias than the engine actually has. A dry run of
the instrument at 34 percent coverage pulled a true 0.556 down to 0.543.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. mean y lands between 0.54 and 0.57, confirming a bias of roughly 2x.
2. mean x lands on 0.500 within noise. This is the control. If x drifts, the
   run is measuring something other than the seed weight and nothing else in
   the output can be trusted.
3. The banded histogram rises monotonically from north to south rather than
   jumping, which is what a linear weight looks like and what a two-zone or
   threshold implementation would not.

RUN IT THREE TIMES. With 500 seeds the standard error on the mean is about
0.013, so one run separates 0.500 from 0.556 at roughly 4 sigma. Three runs
makes it unarguable and also shows the run to run spread, which 5.2's Monte
Carlo needs anyway.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows ELEVATION
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 500
    number_of_tiles 2000
    spacing 1
  }


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
